### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence indicates that the claim of a 99% recovery rate roughly aligns with estimates of the overall infection fatality rate (IFR), which accounts for asymptomatic and undetected infections. However, this figure is not universally true and omits critical context. The confirmed case fatality rate at the time was actually 4.5% (representing a 95.5% recovery rate). Additionally, survival rates were significantly lower for vulnerable groups; the CDC estimated a 94.6% survival rate for individuals over 70, and hospitalized patients had a survival rate of 92.4% by August 2020. Thus, while the 99% figure is plausible when averaging the entire population, it misleads by ignoring the much higher risks faced by older demographics, hospitalized individuals, and those with pre-existing conditions.

### Conclusion
Based on the fact-check, the claim that 99% of people recover from COVID-19 is technically true only when referring to the estimated overall infection fatality rate across the entire population, which includes undetected and asymptomatic cases. However, this statement is highly misleading because it presents a blanket figure that does not apply universally. It excludes vital context, such as the fact that the confirmed case fatality rate was 4.5%, and that survival rates were much lower for older populations (94.6% for those over 70) and hospitalized patients (92.4%). Because the claim is true only under specific conditions and excludes context that would create a significantly different impression of the virus's severity, the final decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The assertion of a 99% recovery rate is misleading because it relies on overall infection fatality rate estimates that include undetected cases, while ignoring much lower recovery rates among specific groups. According to [FactCheck.org](https://www.factcheck.org/2020/07/trumps-false-claim-on-coronavirus-harm/), the confirmed case fatality rate at the time was actually 4.5%, meaning a 95.5% recovery rate for confirmed cases. Furthermore, data published by [Tallahassee Reports](https://tallahasseereports.com/2020/09/26/cdc-releases-updated-covid-19-fatality-rate-data/) shows that the survival rate was lower for older demographics, with the CDC estimating a 94.6% survival rate for individuals over 70 and local data showing a 92.4% survival rate for those 65 and older. Additionally, a study highlighted by [NPR](https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2020/10/20/925441975/studies-point-to-big-drop-in-covid-19-death-rates) revealed that even though hospital survival rates improved, patients admitted with severe symptoms still faced a lower survival rate of 92.4% by August 2020.